The disclosure form shall be signed by a principal executive office of the user and a qualified engineer. The board of public utilities will evaluate the complete disclosure form and data furnished and may require additional information. Within 90 days after full evaluation and acceptance of the data furnished, the board of public utilities shall notify the user of the acceptance thereof.

(b) Categorical baseline requirements. Within 180 days after the effective date of the promulgation or revisions of a national categorical pretreatment standard, all affected existing users must submit to the city the information required by subsections (a)(8) and (9) of this section and:

(1) Name and address of the facility including the name of the operator and owners;

(2) A list of environmental control permits held by or for the facility;

(3) A brief description of the nature, average rate of production, and standard industrial classification of the operation at the facility;

(4) Information showing the measured average daily and maximum daily flow, in gallons per day, to the POTW from regulated process streams and other streams, as necessary to allow use of the combined waste stream formula;

(5) Pretreatment standards applicable to each regulated process and the nature and concentration (or mass, where required) of regulated pollutants in the discharge from each regulated process. The user shall take a minimum of one sample from each regulated process, and the samples shall be representative of daily operations. Samples shall be 24-hour composite samples obtained through flow-proportional composite sampling techniques where feasible, except where a minimum of four grab samples must be used: pH, cyanide, total phenols, oil and grease, sulfide, and volatile organics. Where the user demonstrates to the city that flow-proportional composite sampling is infeasible, samples may be obtained by time-proportional composite sampling techniques; a minimum of four grab samples may instead be used where the user demonstrates to the city that this will provide a reasonable sample of the effluent being discharged.

At least 90 days prior to commencement of discharge, new sources and existing sources that become affected users subsequent to the promulgation of an applicable national categorical pretreatment standard shall submit to the city information on the method of pretreatment they intend to use to meet the applicable pretreatment standard and a report which contains the information listed in subsections (b)(1) through (b)(5) of this section.

(c) Discharge conditions. Wastewater discharges shall be expressly subject to all provisions of this division and all other applicable regulations, user charges and fees established by the city. The city may:

(1) Set unit charges or a schedule of user charges and fees for the wastewater to be discharged to the POTW;

(2) Limit the average and maximum wastewater constituents and characteristics;

(3) Limit the average and maximum rate and time of discharge or make requirements for flow regulations and equalization;

(4) Require the installation and maintenance of inspection and sampling facilities;

(5) Establish specifications for monitoring programs which may include sampling locations, frequency of sampling, number, types and standards for tests and reporting schedule;

